{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"deathly-nondeadly-dorking","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nOutbound sync can only write to one tracker project, and it fails closed on anything else. The create\nclient takes its project from a single value set once at construction\n(`AcliClient.create_issue` uses `self.jira_project`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py:271-327`),\nand the applier scans every outbound mutation pre-flight and raises `CrossProjectTargetError` —\naborting the whole pass — if any update or delete targets a key outside the one configured project\n(`applier.py:217-258`, `_cross_project_targets`, added by bug `626d`). With a mapping that lists\nseveral projects, correct mutations to the other projects would be treated as the misconfiguration\nthat guard exists to catch.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo changes, both narrow.\n\n**Create targets the ticket's project.** The outbound create path resolves the destination through\n`resolve_project` from the mapping module rather than from the client's single construction-time\nvalue. A ticket that resolves to \"not synced\" produces no create mutation at all.\n\n**The guard relaxes from equality to set membership.** `_cross_project_targets` currently compares\neach target key's prefix against one `configured_project` string; it instead checks membership in the\nmapping's project set. This is deliberately the smallest possible weakening: the guard stays\nfail-closed and still aborts pre-flight before any tracker write, so a stale binding pointing at a\nproject the store does not sync is caught exactly as it is today. Only keys the store legitimately\nowns now pass. Creates remain exempt for the existing reason — their `key` is a local-id placeholder,\nnot a real key, so `_JIRA_KEY_RE` does not match them.\n\nThis is the only existing safety property the epic loosens, which is why it is its own story with a\npaired test rather than folded into another change.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/applier.py`\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py`\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_differ.py`\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py`\n- tests under `tests/`\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard's external-service row: a stateful fake transport fed\nproduction-shaped mutations, recording the create calls it receives, because the destination project\ncarried on the call is the contract under test.\n\n- Happy path: a ticket resolving to project `B` in a store whose mapping lists `A` and `B` produces a\n  create call carrying `B`, not the repo default `A`.\n- Contrast case (the guard, both directions in one paired test): an update targeting `B-1` where the\n  mapping lists `A` and `B` proceeds; an update targeting `C-1` still raises\n  `CrossProjectTargetError`. The negative half is what proves the relaxation did not disable the\n  guard.\n- Precondition assertion: the guard is shown to run **pre-flight** — the fake transport records zero\n  writes when the out-of-set mutation raises, proving no partial leak.\n- Edge: a ticket resolving to \"not synced\" produces no outbound mutation of any kind.\n- Collateral invariant: with a single-project mapping the guard behaves exactly as before — an\n  out-of-project key raises, an in-project key passes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An outbound create targets the ticket's own resolved project rather than the client default;\n      checked by a fake transport asserting the project on the recorded create call.\n- [ ] An update inside the configured set proceeds and one outside it raises\n      `CrossProjectTargetError`; checked by a paired test covering both directions.\n- [ ] When the guard raises, no tracker write has occurred; checked by asserting the fake recorded\n      zero writes.\n- [ ] A ticket resolving to not-synced emits no outbound mutation; checked by asserting an empty\n      mutation list for it while a synced sibling produces one.\n- [ ] Single-project behaviour is unchanged; checked by a regression test running the guard against a\n      one-project mapping in both directions.","id":"d19d-7f60-0137-475a","parent_id":"0e68-41eb-5782-4336","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Multi-project outbound dispatch and cross-project guard relaxation to set membership"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786160100332954001,"uuid":"946fc488-d39c-4d5b-9ff6-5a8c1fead7b2"}